<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alessandro Pirzio Biroli</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1877">1877</a>–<a href="/years/1962">1962</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alessandro Pirzio Biroli was an Italian army general. He commanded troops in the Italian conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> in 1935–36 and was governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a>. Appointed military governor of occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> in 1941, he suppressed the uprising there with mass reprisals against civilians. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> later listed him as a war criminal, but he was never extradited.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alessandro-pirzio-biroli.jpg" alt="Alessandro Pirzio Biroli" width="196" height="244" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alessandro_Pirzio_Biroli.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alessandro Pirzio Biroli was born on 23 July 1877. Before his rise as a commander he was also a fencer, and he won a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> medal in the team sabre event at the 1908 Summer Olympics. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he commanded a battalion of the Italian Army on the Macedonian Front and was decorated by the government of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> with the Order of the White Eagle with swords.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1918 he took command of the 8th Bersaglieri Regiment, and between 1921 and 1927 he headed a military mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>. He commanded the Monte Nero Division from 1932 to 1933 and the V Trieste Corps from 1933 to 1935. In the Second Italo-Abyssinian War he led the Eritrean Corps, and he then governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a> province in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-east-africa">Italian East Africa</a> from 1936 to 1937. In 1941 he was made general of the Italian 9th Army.</p><p>After the uprising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> broke out, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> gave him complete civil and military powers there on 25 July 1941, and he became governor that October. Already on 15 July he had ordered his troops to put down the rising with maximum severity, but without unnecessary reprisals. The uprising was suppressed with heavy violence against both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> fighters and civilians.</p><p>His Serbian decoration made him a suitable partner for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chetniks">Chetniks</a>, and on 7 November 1942 at Kolašin the Chetnik commander Pavle Đurišić greeted him as a great friend of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>. On 24 July 1942 the former Ban of the Zeta Banovina, Blažo Đukanović, agreed with him to head the collaborationist Central Nationalist Committee, which was meant to gather collaborators against the Communist Partisans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He served as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> until 1943. According to the Yugoslav historian Pajović, he was personally responsible for numerous executions and for mass terror against the population of Montenegro. Although he stood very high on the list of war criminals of the United Nations War Crimes Commission, he was never tried. He spent his old age in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and died on 20 May 1962.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alessandro Pirzio Biroli” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Italian forces reconquered the towns, and in October 1941 Montenegro was placed under a military governor, General <b>Alessandro Pirzio Biroli</b>, with harsh reprisals.</blockquote><a href="/state/italian-governorate-of-montenegro">Italian governorate of Montenegro</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Campobasso, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.56,14.67&amp;year=1932" aria-label="Campobasso on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4908 682 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alessandro Pirzio Biroli"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5358 952h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5358 952h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.56,14.67&amp;year=1932">What was here in 1932? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
